From: Simon.Urbanek at r-project.org (Simon Urbanek)
Date: Tue, 21 Feb 2012 10:00:41 -0500
Subject: Re: Problems generating a data frame from a table with blank cells
In-Reply-To: <D380B1C6-7AB6-4429-A37E-FFE8370C2119@gmail.com>
References: <FFB05629-4F24-4BD5-8E6D-3EBC4E087E4E@gmail.com> <6794A866-387C-44CE-BC2A-3DA0A684CFF3@r-project.org> <D161F9FF-E1AB-45A2-9250-8647B4D37460@gmail.com> <051A5B35-EF36-4373-BBFD-3CFFECABAA27@r-project.org> <D380B1C6-7AB6-4429-A37E-FFE8370C2119@gmail.com>
Message-ID: <9E86E077-57A7-4B36-B8AB-1931B560B556@r-project.org>

Víctor,

On Feb 21, 2012, at 4:12 AM, Víctor Pascual Cid wrote:

> Thanks, the bug seems to be fixed.
> What do you mean with "note that you are creating pairlist columns instead of vectors"? Not sure I'm understanding what's wrong in that code...
> 

Pretty much the whole idea is wrong. In R data frames consists of column vectors (not lists!). This ensures that each column has a defined type.

However, you are encoding each value separately in a pairlist. Pairlists are typically not used anywhere outside of language constructs and certainly not for data because they are very inefficient (they require linear scan to access anything). Also you are setting names on the elements which is entirely unsupported since data frames have common row names instead.

You example constructs a very odd data frame - it has varying type in rows which is unlikely to work anywhere you pass it (unless coerced into a vector - which some function may do). The correct way to construct it is to allocate the proper vector for each column (such as REXPInteger, REXPString, REXPDouble, ...) and pass a RList of those to createDataFrame - as illustrated in the examples. Note that all vectors must have the same length.

Cheers,
Simon


